Add system-sleep support to the ST accelerometer drivers.

The ST sensor drivers carry no power management at all: struct
i2c_driver/spi_driver have no .pm, and there is no suspend/resume anywhere
under drivers/iio/common/st_sensors/ or drivers/iio/accel/st_accel*.

Across a suspend that actually removes power the chip returns at its reset
defaults.  The configured ODR, full-scale, BDU/DAS and axis enables are
gone, and the data-ready interrupt is left in a state the driver never
programmed.  With a level-triggered DRDY line the threaded handler cannot
claim the interrupt, it re-asserts forever, and the kernel's spurious-
interrupt protection disables it permanently:

  irq 73: nobody cared (try booting with the "irqpoll" option)
  CPU: 0 UID: 0 PID: 200 Comm: irq/73-sc7a20-t

after which the sensor is dead until reboot.

Observed on a Pine64 PineNote (SC7A20 on i2c, level-triggered through the
rockchip GPIO controller) after its first platform "deep" suspend, and
confirmed to reproduce on both a 6.12 BSP kernel and 7.0.11 -- i.e. it is
inherent to the driver, not to one image.

The fix has three parts:

1. Split the register-programming tail of st_sensors_init_sensor() into
   st_sensors_reinit_hw(), which touches no mutex initialisation and no
   device-tree parsing and is therefore safe to repeat.

2. On resume, reprogram from cached state and re-arm DRDY -- latching
   ->hw_irq_trigger BEFORE calling st_sensors_reinit_hw().  This ordering
   is not cosmetic.  reinit_hw() inherits init_sensor()'s "disable DRDY,
   this might still be enabled after reboot" step, and
   st_sensors_set_dataready_irq() assigns ->hw_irq_trigger as a side
   effect, so a later `if (sdata->hw_irq_trigger)` always reads false.
   The re-arm is then skipped: DRDY stays disabled in hardware, the flag
   stays false, and st_sensors_irq_thread() takes its "spurious IRQ"
   branch and returns IRQ_NONE on every interrupt.  On a level-triggered
   line that reproduces the exact storm the patch is meant to prevent --
   measured on a PineNote as ~100,000 interrupts in the second after
   resume before the kernel disabled the line.

3. Mask the interrupt across the transition with disable_irq()/
   enable_irq(), so the handler cannot run against a chip that is still
   at its reset defaults.  A ->pm_irq_disabled flag keeps the pairing
   exact so the depth counter cannot unbalance if suspend returns early.

A note on (3): an earlier revision of this patch carried only parts 1 and
3 and still stormed, which was attributed to resume_device_irqs() running
at the noirq stage, before driver .resume callbacks.  That revision
contained the (2) ordering bug, which is sufficient on its own to explain
the failure, so the noirq argument should be treated as unproven
motivation for (3) rather than as a measured result.  Part 3 is retained
because masking a level-triggered line across a power-removing transition
is correct regardless, but the defect actually measured was (2).

